WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.029 --> 00:00:09.260
Thank you all for coming.

00:00:09.260 --> 00:00:11.889
Tonight we're going to talk about a very serious
subject.

00:00:11.889 --> 00:00:18.750
Um, the situation that we face with antibiotic
resistant bacteria.

00:00:18.750 --> 00:00:23.630
Antibiotics were once the silver bullet that
seemed to be able to cure just about everything.

00:00:23.630 --> 00:00:29.840
Now we look at 23,000 antibiotic resistant
bacterial infections every year.

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:34.340
So let me introduce you to our panelists.

00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:39.129
Our first participant is the Director of the
Wisconsin Institute for Discovery at the University

00:00:39.129 --> 00:00:40.129
of Wisconsin-Madison.

00:00:40.129 --> 00:00:42.949
She was a science advisor to President Barack
Obama.

00:00:42.949 --> 00:00:47.780
Please welcome Jo Handelsman.

00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:53.690
Our next participant is a professor at Rockefeller
University where he is head of the Laboratory

00:00:53.690 --> 00:00:57.409
of Bacterial Pathogenesis and Immunology.

00:00:57.409 --> 00:01:01.940
Please welcome Vincent Fischetti.

00:01:01.940 --> 00:01:07.909
Our next participant is an associate professor
of Immunobiology and Microbial Pathogenesis

00:01:07.909 --> 00:01:09.420
at The Salk Institute.

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:14.320
Please welcome Janelle Ayres.

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:18.680
Our next participant is the Evnin Associate
Professor at Rockefeller University.

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:21.430
Please welcome Sean Brady.

00:01:21.430 --> 00:01:27.281
and finally the Singer Professor of Medicine
in Microbiology, and the Director of the Human

00:01:27.281 --> 00:01:30.920
Microbiome Program at NYU School of Medicine.

00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:32.640
Please welcome Martin Blaser.

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:40.039
Uh so, I thought that maybe a way to start
would be to show a video.

00:01:40.039 --> 00:01:45.640
This was an experiment that was done at Harvard
where basically scientists created at sort

00:01:45.640 --> 00:01:53.289
of gigantic petri dish, sort of kind of the
size of an air hockey table basically, and

00:01:53.289 --> 00:02:01.649
they seeded it with bacteria on either side
and then basically laced it with antibiotics.

00:02:01.649 --> 00:02:06.140
Starting at the edge with pretty mild levels
and then as you go further in, it gets more

00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:13.240
and more deadly until the central band has
a thousand times the lethal dose for Ecoli.

00:02:13.240 --> 00:02:15.069
It's really kind of mind blowing.

00:02:15.069 --> 00:02:20.709
We're going to see the bacteria, so just sort
of hanging out there and now they're multiplying.

00:02:20.709 --> 00:02:26.920
This is sort of time lapse, so when they hit
that point, what they're encountering that

00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:29.349
as antibiotics and then how are they getting
past it?

00:02:29.349 --> 00:02:31.819
I mean you can see them.

00:02:31.819 --> 00:02:37.940
It takes a little bit of time and what they're
waiting for is growth of one or a very small

00:02:37.940 --> 00:02:41.890
number of cells in the population that are
already resistant.

00:02:41.890 --> 00:02:47.590
And so although there were many, many billions
of cells crossing the plate, probably one

00:02:47.590 --> 00:02:51.482
in a million would have resistance to the
antibiotic, so once those started dividing

00:02:51.482 --> 00:02:52.482
and-

00:02:52.482 --> 00:02:56.860
So they're dividing and one in a million just
happens to gain this power to get past it?

00:02:56.860 --> 00:02:58.540
Well, they always had the power.

00:02:58.540 --> 00:03:03.250
It was preexisting in the population, but
then when the whole population is, the rest

00:03:03.250 --> 00:03:07.370
of the population is stopped by the antibiotic
because they're inhibited by it.

00:03:07.370 --> 00:03:12.830
Then those few that are resistant start proliferating
and then they take over and that's exactly

00:03:12.830 --> 00:03:18.650
what it looks like in similar terms when it's
in the body, you know, you take an antibiotic

00:03:18.650 --> 00:03:23.000
and most of the bacteria will die, but there
will be preexisting mutants in the population

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:24.170
that are resistant.

00:03:24.170 --> 00:03:25.170
That's great.

00:03:25.170 --> 00:03:26.181
Well not great, but it's amazing.

00:03:26.181 --> 00:03:28.709
It is definitely not great.

00:03:28.709 --> 00:03:29.709
Yeah, no it didn't.

00:03:29.709 --> 00:03:30.709
I know, I know.

00:03:30.709 --> 00:03:31.709
Don't get me wrong.

00:03:31.709 --> 00:03:38.489
We think of antibiotics as, as the sort of
heroic triumph of science.

00:03:38.489 --> 00:03:42.540
I mean, how did, how did we get to enjoy the
benefits of antibiotics?

00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:45.140
I mean, how did that begin?

00:03:45.140 --> 00:03:51.659
Well, it started with a chance discovery from
a by Sir Alexander Fleming.

00:03:51.659 --> 00:03:59.040
In the UK, who found a fungus on his plate
that was clearly inhibiting growth of staphylococcus

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:05.860
on, on the Petri dish, and he recognized what
it was that this was a compound that was diffusing

00:04:05.860 --> 00:04:10.591
into the Auger and determined that it was
what we now know of as penicillin, but it

00:04:10.591 --> 00:04:16.519
was many years before it can be used in any
kind of broad scale way because he discovered

00:04:16.519 --> 00:04:22.810
that in 1929 and by the start of World War
II, we still, we're not using antibiotics

00:04:22.810 --> 00:04:25.410
and they weren't in general use.

00:04:25.410 --> 00:04:26.410
Why not?

00:04:26.410 --> 00:04:29.259
I mean you, you discover a drug, you know,
put it into practice.

00:04:29.259 --> 00:04:30.889
I mean, what was the holdup?

00:04:30.889 --> 00:04:32.680
They couldn't make enough of it.

00:04:32.680 --> 00:04:37.650
That fungus produced some but not enough to
go into large scale production.

00:04:37.650 --> 00:04:44.080
And so during the war a scientist named Ken
Raper was worked for the USDA in Peoria and

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:50.169
he decided as a war effort to put out the
call for penicillin producing strains of penicillium

00:04:50.169 --> 00:04:51.169
mold.

00:04:51.169 --> 00:04:56.150
So he told everybody in Peoria to collect
as many fruits and vegetables with that green

00:04:56.150 --> 00:05:01.070
blue fuzzy thing that you see on your bread
and fruit to bring those to his lab.

00:05:01.070 --> 00:05:05.669
And people did and he had this large collection
and it turned out it was his own technician

00:05:05.669 --> 00:05:08.800
who has gone down in history, as Moldy Mary
now.

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:15.780
Her name was actually Mary Hunt and she brought
in the winning cantaloupe and it had a strain

00:05:15.780 --> 00:05:21.740
of penicillium mold that produces more penicillin
than any other natural strain to this day.

00:05:21.740 --> 00:05:25.919
And so they started, they moved it right into
commercial production and started pumping

00:05:25.919 --> 00:05:30.350
out large amounts of penicillin and they have
enough to be able to ship it the penicillin

00:05:30.350 --> 00:05:31.910
to the troops in Europe.

00:05:31.910 --> 00:05:35.980
And so World War Two was the first war in
which more people died directly of bullets

00:05:35.980 --> 00:05:39.090
and bombs than the infections that accompanied
them.

00:05:39.090 --> 00:05:40.090
Wow.

00:05:40.090 --> 00:05:45.170
So, and then once, once the war is over, then
antibiotic start to become more of just a

00:05:45.170 --> 00:05:48.600
general medicine for the public in general,
right?

00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:49.600
Yeah.

00:05:49.600 --> 00:05:53.910
And at the same time there was interest in
soil bacteria that produced antibiotics.

00:05:53.910 --> 00:05:58.180
And, and then after the war there was just
this explosion of knowledge of people, culturing

00:05:58.180 --> 00:06:03.580
organisms from the soil, screening them for
antibiotics and then moving into production.

00:06:03.580 --> 00:06:08.979
And so we had dozens of antibiotics coming
onto the market in the next decades.

00:06:08.979 --> 00:06:14.240
Sean, I mean, how would you sort of describe
like the sort of, the overall benefit of these

00:06:14.240 --> 00:06:17.850
discoveries of penicillin and some of these
other early antibiotics?

00:06:17.850 --> 00:06:23.970
I mean, what I mean overall, like in terms
of lives saved or someone, what are we looking

00:06:23.970 --> 00:06:26.380
at in terms of the scale of this?

00:06:26.380 --> 00:06:30.400
I think one of the figures that penicillin
alone has saved 100 million lives.

00:06:30.400 --> 00:06:31.600
And that's penicillin alone.

00:06:31.600 --> 00:06:32.600
Yeah.

00:06:32.600 --> 00:06:35.289
So if you think about that single picture
that Joe talked to you, you see it in almost

00:06:35.289 --> 00:06:36.600
any microbiology textbook.

00:06:36.600 --> 00:06:40.889
That image has probably saved more lives than
anything in the history of science.

00:06:40.889 --> 00:06:45.050
So you want one kind of thing in your office,
you should hang that picture as a scientist

00:06:45.050 --> 00:06:48.780
because it's made a larger impact on human
health than anything but, but that, that whole

00:06:48.780 --> 00:06:50.960
discovery, even today we're still using those
antibiotics.

00:06:50.960 --> 00:06:51.960
So.

00:06:51.960 --> 00:06:55.430
So that's the initial discovery, then you
think about almost everything that came out

00:06:55.430 --> 00:06:59.349
of what we call the golden age of antibiotics,
the forties, fifties, and sixties.

00:06:59.349 --> 00:07:02.220
So people were finding things not just on
cantaloupe but in other-

00:07:02.220 --> 00:07:06.580
They are culturing soil bacteria largely and
finding antibiotics.

00:07:06.580 --> 00:07:09.879
Almost every class of antibiotics that we
use today was discovered in that that time

00:07:09.879 --> 00:07:10.879
period.

00:07:10.879 --> 00:07:16.680
We have relied on antibiotic defense really
of those molecules and continually using versions

00:07:16.680 --> 00:07:17.711
of those molecules up until today.

00:07:17.711 --> 00:07:20.880
And that's why we're in the position we are
today.

00:07:20.880 --> 00:07:26.020
We, we've largely ceased discovering antibiotics
after the golden age, the late sixties, early

00:07:26.020 --> 00:07:27.020
seventies.

00:07:27.020 --> 00:07:28.020
Because we thought we were done.

00:07:28.020 --> 00:07:29.020
We thought we had solutions to these problems.

00:07:29.020 --> 00:07:31.000
That's how good those initial discoveries
were.

00:07:31.000 --> 00:07:34.340
How, how much of an impact they made on human
health.

00:07:34.340 --> 00:07:38.780
What's your sense of like when it started
to become clear that things weren't going

00:07:38.780 --> 00:07:39.780
so well?

00:07:39.780 --> 00:07:40.780
Like when?

00:07:40.780 --> 00:07:45.389
When do you think that the sort of scientific
medical community said, I think we have a

00:07:45.389 --> 00:07:46.389
problem?

00:07:46.389 --> 00:07:47.440
Well that happened pretty quickly.

00:07:47.440 --> 00:07:52.720
I mean we were seeing resistant organisms
to penicillin early on.

00:07:52.720 --> 00:07:54.039
It was, it started

00:07:54.039 --> 00:07:56.210
Like a matter of a few years after?

00:07:56.210 --> 00:07:57.830
Probably a year or two

00:07:57.830 --> 00:07:59.479
After the introduction of penicillin.

00:07:59.479 --> 00:08:05.090
Exactly we already started to see early stages
of resistance, but you know, was it an organism

00:08:05.090 --> 00:08:09.960
here, an organism there, but, but it was occurring
at that time and it's been occurring at an

00:08:09.960 --> 00:08:13.710
accelerated rate since then.

00:08:13.710 --> 00:08:15.580
Right.

00:08:15.580 --> 00:08:25.270
So Marty, what do you think is, what would
you say would be like one of the main factors

00:08:25.270 --> 00:08:30.759
that explain sort of how we got to this point
in terms of resistance?

00:08:30.759 --> 00:08:38.190
Like what are we doing that is causing all
of these bacteria now to be just so dangerous?

00:08:38.190 --> 00:08:45.750
So, uh, the short answer is that Darwin was
right and that is that there is survival of

00:08:45.750 --> 00:08:46.750
the fittest.

00:08:46.750 --> 00:08:47.750
It's selection.

00:08:47.750 --> 00:08:55.940
We are using antibiotics in such magnitude
because of the miraculous nature of antibiotics,

00:08:55.940 --> 00:09:02.550
both the public and the profession says, well,
why don't we just treat this person with antibiotics

00:09:02.550 --> 00:09:05.190
even if their symptoms are minimal.

00:09:05.190 --> 00:09:11.760
So there's enormous pressure, selective pressure
of antibiotic use and it's just, it's just

00:09:11.760 --> 00:09:15.510
a mathematical certainty that there'll be
resistance, but it's not linear.

00:09:15.510 --> 00:09:20.180
It's, it's geometric because of the properties
of bacteria growing.

00:09:20.180 --> 00:09:27.560
Yes, but you have to remember that bacteria
come, most of them come from the soil and

00:09:27.560 --> 00:09:31.770
antibiotics are in the soil, so they've learned
for millions of years how to deal with antibiotics.

00:09:31.770 --> 00:09:34.510
So the systems are there for as long as you.

00:09:34.510 --> 00:09:39.170
If you expose them to antibiotics, those systems
become heightened, then become resistant.

00:09:39.170 --> 00:09:47.250
So they, they've seen these drugs or similar
drugs or antibiotics type molecules for hundreds

00:09:47.250 --> 00:09:50.020
and hundreds of thousands, millions of years.

00:09:50.020 --> 00:09:57.340
Marty, you, you've also been talking a lot
and writing a lot about the fact that our

00:09:57.340 --> 00:10:04.560
antibiotics are not precision weapons that
you know, you use them against Ecoli, MRSA,

00:10:04.560 --> 00:10:08.160
and so on, but that's not the only thing that's
going to affect.

00:10:08.160 --> 00:10:14.340
Yeah, so so antibiotics came of age when we
were, when we were really trying to eliminate

00:10:14.340 --> 00:10:19.710
these bad pathogens, but no one really considered
what was the effect of the antibiotics or

00:10:19.710 --> 00:10:24.440
the normal bacteria living in the body, the
normal bacteria that we call the microbiome,

00:10:24.440 --> 00:10:31.380
but now it's clear that that when you take
an antibiotic for a skin infection or lung

00:10:31.380 --> 00:10:35.830
or urinary tract infection, that antibiotic
is getting everywhere in the body and it is

00:10:35.830 --> 00:10:38.860
selecting for resistant organisms in that
body.

00:10:38.860 --> 00:10:43.890
That's suppressing some organisms and other
organisms are coming up and maybe some organisms

00:10:43.890 --> 00:10:46.120
are becoming extinct as well.

00:10:46.120 --> 00:10:49.230
So these are organisms that we might actually
depend on.

00:10:49.230 --> 00:10:51.250
It might be actually beneficial for us.

00:10:51.250 --> 00:10:57.310
And so in fact we know that one of the main
defenses against infection are our residual.

00:10:57.310 --> 00:11:02.440
Our normal organisms there, there, there,
the coast guard, they are protecting against

00:11:02.440 --> 00:11:03.440
invaders.

00:11:03.440 --> 00:11:08.170
They don't want to share their turf and 50
years ago it was shown that if you pretreat

00:11:08.170 --> 00:11:14.150
mice or other laboratory animals with antibiotics
and then give them a pathogen like Salmonella,

00:11:14.150 --> 00:11:19.020
the, the level of Salmonella that it takes
to kill the mouse goes down by four logs,

00:11:19.020 --> 00:11:21.650
you know, by 10,000 falls.

00:11:21.650 --> 00:11:27.560
So Sean, I mean someone might say like, well
we have all these gigantic pharmaceutical

00:11:27.560 --> 00:11:28.560
companies.

00:11:28.560 --> 00:11:31.810
There's lots of money that they can throw
at the problem.

00:11:31.810 --> 00:11:36.170
You know, there was penicillin and then there
were other things I can think of mycin and

00:11:36.170 --> 00:11:38.980
you know, you know, science marches on.

00:11:38.980 --> 00:11:43.170
So we've got like more in the pipeline, right?

00:11:43.170 --> 00:11:44.170
That's the unfortunate thing.

00:11:44.170 --> 00:11:45.810
We have almost nothing in the pipeline.

00:11:45.810 --> 00:11:46.860
Almost nothing.

00:11:46.860 --> 00:11:49.460
You can, you can ascribe that to a number
of different reasons.

00:11:49.460 --> 00:11:52.310
We don't get in a crisis because of one thing,
but we get in it because many things came

00:11:52.310 --> 00:11:54.650
together that we probably didn't foresee.

00:11:54.650 --> 00:11:57.670
One of them being that our first round of
antibiotics worked so well right?

00:11:57.670 --> 00:12:01.590
That golden age of antibiotics when we were
describing them, people thought we were done

00:12:01.590 --> 00:12:08.180
and so so over the next ensuing 30 years antibody
discovery programs, both in academic and industrial

00:12:08.180 --> 00:12:12.141
settings largely shut down and so there are
almost no pharmaceutical industries that are

00:12:12.141 --> 00:12:15.750
putting at least the effort they used to put
in to finding antibiotics.

00:12:15.750 --> 00:12:20.250
The second thing is then if we'd been using
antibiotics, the same ones for 30 years, that

00:12:20.250 --> 00:12:21.660
means they don't cost us anything anymore.

00:12:21.660 --> 00:12:22.960
They're all generics.

00:12:22.960 --> 00:12:27.600
You can get an antibiotic for somewhere between
free and twenty cents a day in many parts

00:12:27.600 --> 00:12:32.300
of the world, so now you have an infrastructure
that doesn't exist and you have a financial

00:12:32.300 --> 00:12:35.350
structure that doesn't support the development
of antibiotics.

00:12:35.350 --> 00:12:40.310
So we are at a certain point trying to figure
out how to restart that pharmaceutical industry

00:12:40.310 --> 00:12:42.830
and how to make it worthwhile to restart it.

00:12:42.830 --> 00:12:45.210
Have to be some major things changed.

00:12:45.210 --> 00:12:49.810
It's in direct competition with chronic disease
which is much more lucrative for the companies

00:12:49.810 --> 00:12:53.350
because it drug you take for the rest of your
life is obviously going to make them more

00:12:53.350 --> 00:12:58.990
money than a drug you take for five days and
then stop, and so even even now with the crisis

00:12:58.990 --> 00:13:01.603
that we all know we're in, very few companies
want to move back into that area.

00:13:01.603 --> 00:13:08.030
And what they're doing is taking a drug that
worked, became, an organism, becomes resistant,

00:13:08.030 --> 00:13:10.800
and they just make a modification on that
drug.

00:13:10.800 --> 00:13:14.860
It's cheaper for them to do that than to start
from the beginning and now the virus can become

00:13:14.860 --> 00:13:16.350
resistant much more rapidly.

00:13:16.350 --> 00:13:20.270
So they work for maybe a year or two and then
they can't use them anymore.

00:13:20.270 --> 00:13:21.270
Marty.

00:13:21.270 --> 00:13:28.000
And then there's yet another problem and that
is that bacteria don't respect borders and

00:13:28.000 --> 00:13:35.950
so what that means is that if, if a resistant
organism arises in another country like India

00:13:35.950 --> 00:13:40.800
or China, it doesn't take too long for it
to come over here and because antibiotics

00:13:40.800 --> 00:13:45.610
are so inexpensive and because people think
that they're so miraculous.

00:13:45.610 --> 00:13:49.910
In many of these countries, people are able
to get antibiotics over the counter, no prescription

00:13:49.910 --> 00:13:52.000
necessary.

00:13:52.000 --> 00:13:57.200
Parents are giving their kids 10 courses of
antibiotics a year in, in some recent studies

00:13:57.200 --> 00:14:02.420
funded by the Gates Foundation, tremendous
antibiotic pressure, very low cost, but somebody's

00:14:02.420 --> 00:14:05.200
making money on those antibiotics.

00:14:05.200 --> 00:14:09.910
Resistant organisms are arising and then there
are the crossing all over the world,

00:14:09.910 --> 00:14:14.140
So this, so this sort of cheap marketplace
of antibiotics over the counter and so on

00:14:14.140 --> 00:14:15.820
is even helping to drive on-

00:14:15.820 --> 00:14:20.000
The whole antibiotic market is broken.

00:14:20.000 --> 00:14:24.960
Antibiotics are in one sense too cheap and
and, and are therefore overused and abused.

00:14:24.960 --> 00:14:30.950
And on the other hand there's no incentive
to create new antibiotics that we want to

00:14:30.950 --> 00:14:38.030
keep and put in reserve for important infections,
which won't affect tens of millions of people

00:14:38.030 --> 00:14:40.930
so that there isn't that market.

00:14:40.930 --> 00:14:46.760
So the market, the economic model for antibiotics
is just broken.

00:14:46.760 --> 00:14:48.860
Just, just to put a number on that, right?

00:14:48.860 --> 00:14:49.860
Yeah.

00:14:49.860 --> 00:14:53.080
So the most recent, they're going to differ
a little bit, but let's say the six months,

00:14:53.080 --> 00:14:56.910
recent antibiotics that came to market made
about $10,000,000 each last year.

00:14:56.910 --> 00:14:57.980
10, 10 million each.

00:14:57.980 --> 00:14:58.980
Right?

00:14:58.980 --> 00:15:02.280
Okay, that seems like a lot of money, but
just let's say you've done all the clinical

00:15:02.280 --> 00:15:06.450
trials you need and now you need to synthesize
a production scale an antibiotic.

00:15:06.450 --> 00:15:08.560
It's $150,000,000 investment, right?

00:15:08.560 --> 00:15:12.570
And the reason these things make a little
money is, is you don't want to use them.

00:15:12.570 --> 00:15:14.330
You don't want to use in this frontline defense,
right?

00:15:14.330 --> 00:15:18.160
You want to put them in reserve until you
absolutely need them.

00:15:18.160 --> 00:15:19.220
And so where's the incentive?

00:15:19.220 --> 00:15:23.680
If, if forget the hundreds of millions you
put into development, just to make the thing

00:15:23.680 --> 00:15:27.230
costs you 10 times which you can sell it for,
sell it for a year.

00:15:27.230 --> 00:15:32.190
We really have to rethink how we, how we market
these things, how we as a community decide

00:15:32.190 --> 00:15:36.790
we're going to put antibiotics in reserve
and put an upfront and of realization that

00:15:36.790 --> 00:15:38.340
these things are there.

00:15:38.340 --> 00:15:41.100
We need to pay for them as a community because
we're going to need them some day.

00:15:41.100 --> 00:15:45.810
Alright, so let's, let's brighten things up
a bit by like actually, you know, you folks

00:15:45.810 --> 00:15:47.010
are actually working on things.

00:15:47.010 --> 00:15:48.170
So maybe we'll start.

00:15:48.170 --> 00:15:51.160
We'll start with antibiotics themselves, with
new antibiotics.

00:15:51.160 --> 00:15:52.740
So with Jo and Sean's work.

00:15:52.740 --> 00:16:00.350
So, so, so you've been going back to the soil,
the soil that brought us all these original

00:16:00.350 --> 00:16:01.350
drugs.

00:16:01.350 --> 00:16:03.110
You think you think there are more there for
us to find?

00:16:03.110 --> 00:16:04.110
I do.

00:16:04.110 --> 00:16:10.450
I'm so, for a long time I went to other methods
for antibiotic discovery and you'll hear about

00:16:10.450 --> 00:16:15.700
some of those that Sean's developed soon,
but the reason I did that was that there were

00:16:15.700 --> 00:16:20.490
some references from the nineties that said
that the soil was mined.

00:16:20.490 --> 00:16:26.500
It was fully tapped and I've gone back to
the data and I can't find the data and so

00:16:26.500 --> 00:16:31.680
now I question whether that's really true
because in the ensuing decades my lab just

00:16:31.680 --> 00:16:37.000
spontaneously discovered antibiotics, novel
antibiotics from soil that hadn't been discovered

00:16:37.000 --> 00:16:38.560
and we weren't even looking in some cases.

00:16:38.560 --> 00:16:39.640
And so I.

00:16:39.640 --> 00:16:44.440
It just occurred to me one day, wait a minute,
it's not mined if we're finding them and so

00:16:44.440 --> 00:16:48.950
that's the approach we're taking is going
back and asking what is the frequency of new

00:16:48.950 --> 00:16:49.950
compounds?

00:16:49.950 --> 00:16:55.130
There was one paper that said the rediscovery
rate would be 99 percent, so if you found

00:16:55.130 --> 00:16:59.270
100 compounds, 99 of them would be already
known.

00:16:59.270 --> 00:17:05.069
Well that's actually not so bad if it's true
because we can look at a lot more than 100

00:17:05.069 --> 00:17:07.319
compounds with today's methods, but.

00:17:07.319 --> 00:17:12.190
But I'm not even sure that that's true because
it wasn't really based on at least published

00:17:12.190 --> 00:17:13.190
data.

00:17:13.190 --> 00:17:17.010
Maybe somebody in a pharmaceutical company
has the data, but we haven't seen it.

00:17:17.010 --> 00:17:22.180
So are there particular places that you like
to go look for new antibiotics?

00:17:22.180 --> 00:17:26.930
In a particular soil that is you like or is
