00:17:26.930 --> 00:17:32.020
Well, we're looking at across the world, so
we have a worldwide network of undergraduate

00:17:32.020 --> 00:17:34.570
students.

00:17:34.570 --> 00:17:38.060
Undergraduates who are a fantastic and very
creative workforce.

00:17:38.060 --> 00:17:42.000
So we developed a course that is known as
the Small World Initiative and it's taught

00:17:42.000 --> 00:17:47.560
in 15 countries and all over the United States
and about 10,000 students a year take the

00:17:47.560 --> 00:17:52.940
course and they dig up soil from whatever
environment is interesting to them and they

00:17:52.940 --> 00:17:58.050
come up with more interesting reasons than
I ever would for why an environment is interesting.

00:17:58.050 --> 00:18:01.760
And and so they have this great variety of
soils.

00:18:01.760 --> 00:18:06.320
They're isolating very interesting antibiotic
producing organisms and now we have to go

00:18:06.320 --> 00:18:09.680
into the next stage which is figuring out
what antibiotics are produced.

00:18:09.680 --> 00:18:15.890
So we think that if we have 10,000 students,
each one gets at least 10 antibiotic producing

00:18:15.890 --> 00:18:18.110
organisms per year.

00:18:18.110 --> 00:18:19.940
That's a lot of candidates.

00:18:19.940 --> 00:18:25.510
And so if we can crank through enough of them,
even if that one percent rediscovery or 99

00:18:25.510 --> 00:18:30.500
percent rediscovery rate is correct, we still
have a lot of new compounds to look at.

00:18:30.500 --> 00:18:38.240
So Sean, what kind of approach are you taking
to searching for these, these new antibiotics?

00:18:38.240 --> 00:18:44.880
So about 20 years ago now, I guess, Jo and
a few other people were thinking along these

00:18:44.880 --> 00:18:49.790
ideas, thinking about is there a reservoir
in soil still of, of natural products and,

00:18:49.790 --> 00:18:55.470
and the thing that that percolated to the
top of the thinking of these people was that

00:18:55.470 --> 00:19:00.220
there's data from even longer ago, maybe 120
years ago that it appears we don't culture

00:19:00.220 --> 00:19:03.960
most of the bacteria out of the environment,
that actually the bugs we've been playing

00:19:03.960 --> 00:19:07.000
with represent a small fraction of the bacteria
in the environment.

00:19:07.000 --> 00:19:13.570
So let me ask you, so if you like take a sample
of a little sample of soil, first of all,

00:19:13.570 --> 00:19:17.670
like how many microbes are in there and how
much DNA are you talking about that you're

00:19:17.670 --> 00:19:19.240
looking at from all of them?

00:19:19.240 --> 00:19:23.150
So it depends on whose numbers, let's say
is there's thousands, maybe 10,000 different

00:19:23.150 --> 00:19:26.870
microbes of which we culture about one percent.

00:19:26.870 --> 00:19:27.870
Just one percent,.

00:19:27.870 --> 00:19:28.870
Just one percent.

00:19:28.870 --> 00:19:32.000
And again, people have done better nowadays,
but they don't solve the other problem, which

00:19:32.000 --> 00:19:35.820
is even if we can culture bacteria, we don't
turn on their genes.

00:19:35.820 --> 00:19:36.820
Right?

00:19:36.820 --> 00:19:40.270
So even if you can bring bacteria in the lab,
they don't know how to turn on the genes,

00:19:40.270 --> 00:19:41.990
they're gonna make antibiotics for us.

00:19:41.990 --> 00:19:45.660
And so, so what we want to do is just look
at their DNA and you can get huge amounts

00:19:45.660 --> 00:19:51.970
of DNA at least in the context of molecular
biology out of a single gram of soil.

00:19:51.970 --> 00:19:55.430
And so it's the coming together of this idea
that we can culture bacteria.

00:19:55.430 --> 00:19:57.120
We can sequence their genomes and we can.

00:19:57.120 --> 00:20:01.140
We can mess with genes, right genes in ways
that we can turn them on that really allows

00:20:01.140 --> 00:20:06.700
you to untapped this reservoir that's been
tapped or untapped.

00:20:06.700 --> 00:20:07.810
So.

00:20:07.810 --> 00:20:15.930
So Vincent, I wanted to, to kind of shift
gears here and look at a way of dealing with

00:20:15.930 --> 00:20:21.600
bacteria that's totally doesn't involve antibiotics
at all.

00:20:21.600 --> 00:20:28.470
Um, there's, and this is, this is kind of
a long running idea of basically sending the

00:20:28.470 --> 00:20:30.430
enemies of bacteria against them.

00:20:30.430 --> 00:20:35.480
I mean, can you explain the idea of this kind
of approach?

00:20:35.480 --> 00:20:37.140
What was sometimes called Phage therapy?

00:20:37.140 --> 00:20:40.710
Well phage therapy actually started before
antibiotic therapy.

00:20:40.710 --> 00:20:45.290
So, um, it was discovered by D'Herelle about
100 years ago.

00:20:45.290 --> 00:20:52.950
He discovered a, he had a vessel in the, it
was cloudy with bacteria and suddenly it disappeared,

00:20:52.950 --> 00:20:54.700
just disappeared in his eyes.

00:20:54.700 --> 00:20:58.700
And he said some things in there that killed
the bacteria, figured out that it was, it

00:20:58.700 --> 00:21:04.660
was a virus, a virus that only infected bacteria,
bacteria phage, it's called.

00:21:04.660 --> 00:21:12.350
And that started a revolution at the time
to use phage to control infection.

00:21:12.350 --> 00:21:14.050
It was well before antibiotics.

00:21:14.050 --> 00:21:17.460
So these, so these viruses, they're back,
they're known as bacteria phage.

00:21:17.460 --> 00:21:21.570
So what are we looking at?

00:21:21.570 --> 00:21:22.860
So the blue thing is bacteria.

00:21:22.860 --> 00:21:29.310
The blue thing is the bacteria and the ring
around that is the cell wall of bacteria.

00:21:29.310 --> 00:21:34.510
When it attaches, it injects its DNA into
the cell and once that DNA gets into the cell,

00:21:34.510 --> 00:21:38.760
it takes over the self for the production,
a new virus particles and once those virus

00:21:38.760 --> 00:21:42.040
particles are produced, the phage have a problem.

00:21:42.040 --> 00:21:46.620
They have to get out of that organism and
they solve the problem by producing an enzyme

00:21:46.620 --> 00:21:49.410
called the lysine that drills a hole in the
cell wall.

00:21:49.410 --> 00:21:53.500
And since the pressure inside the bacteria
is greater than the external environment,

00:21:53.500 --> 00:21:59.260
the organism explodes and releases the bacteria
phage that had been produced in the environment.

00:21:59.260 --> 00:22:04.620
And that's phage therapy using those phage
to kill the organism directly.

00:22:04.620 --> 00:22:10.200
What we've done is now taking that enzyme,
the specific enzyme that drills a hole in

00:22:10.200 --> 00:22:15.980
the cell wall, we can produce it recombinantly,
and when you add that enzyme externally, it

00:22:15.980 --> 00:22:20.940
does precisely what it did from the inside,
drills a hole in the wall membrane externalizes

00:22:20.940 --> 00:22:29.040
and kills the organism, so we've developed
the enzyme that the phage now uses to release

00:22:29.040 --> 00:22:30.810
its progeny phage.

00:22:30.810 --> 00:22:36.630
You could use phage themselves and that's
called phage therapy as a means to control

00:22:36.630 --> 00:22:41.170
bacteria, but you can use the enzyme to to
accomplish the same thing.

00:22:41.170 --> 00:22:46.470
And there are particular species of phages
that can go after particular species of bacteria?

00:22:46.470 --> 00:22:51.120
The very specific, that's the problem with
phage therapy is that they're highly specific

00:22:51.120 --> 00:22:53.880
for the organism that you're going after.

00:22:53.880 --> 00:22:59.240
So in order to kill, for instance of Staph
Aureus, you'll need to produce a cocktail

00:22:59.240 --> 00:23:06.020
of maybe five or six or 10 or 15 phage to
get around the chance of organisms becoming

00:23:06.020 --> 00:23:09.810
resistant because the bacteria become resistant
very rapidly to phage.

00:23:09.810 --> 00:23:13.000
So they getting resistant to the phages as
well.

00:23:13.000 --> 00:23:14.500
Antibiotics, they're just evolving,

00:23:14.500 --> 00:23:15.500
But that's.

00:23:15.500 --> 00:23:17.160
That's the normal system.

00:23:17.160 --> 00:23:21.580
The phage are trying to get into the organism,
the bacteria trying to keep them out.

00:23:21.580 --> 00:23:24.580
So that balance has been going on for a billion
years.

00:23:24.580 --> 00:23:29.440
Nobody wants to win that war, phage that want
to win because if they win, all is gone.

00:23:29.440 --> 00:23:31.460
If the bacteria when.

00:23:31.460 --> 00:23:36.950
Well they can't get enough DNA into them to
to, to, to modulate their, their, their DNA

00:23:36.950 --> 00:23:37.950
themselves.

00:23:37.950 --> 00:23:38.950
Right.

00:23:38.950 --> 00:23:39.950
Because bacteria are taking in.

00:23:39.950 --> 00:23:41.330
They are taking in DNA and so they need that.

00:23:41.330 --> 00:23:48.260
That acquisition of phage DNA that doesn't
kill them, that allows them to pick up genes

00:23:48.260 --> 00:23:50.960
if they, allows them to survive much more
rapidly.

00:23:50.960 --> 00:23:56.080
So then there's this molecule that phage make,
this enzyme called lysine, and so you want

00:23:56.080 --> 00:24:01.800
to just try just using lysine rather than
the whole virus.

00:24:01.800 --> 00:24:05.260
We've been using lysine for almost 20 years
now.

00:24:05.260 --> 00:24:10.510
We have lysine and the beauty of lysine is
that they are very specific for the organism.

00:24:10.510 --> 00:24:13.100
We don't see resistance, we'd never seen resistance.

00:24:13.100 --> 00:24:17.300
We've been doing this for 20 years that they
cannot become resistant to lysines because

00:24:17.300 --> 00:24:21.871
they'd have to remodel their cell walls, so
it would take them a very long time to become

00:24:21.871 --> 00:24:23.000
resistance.

00:24:23.000 --> 00:24:27.850
Probably hundreds of years before they become
resistant to actual lysines.

00:24:27.850 --> 00:24:34.530
So those are anthrax organisms and we've added
lysine to them and you could see what happens

00:24:34.530 --> 00:24:35.530
to them.

00:24:35.530 --> 00:24:38.760
This is real time.

00:24:38.760 --> 00:24:43.050
They just explode and disappear.

00:24:43.050 --> 00:24:48.140
So you can take 10 billion organisms in a
test tube and add up five few micrograms of

00:24:48.140 --> 00:24:52.930
lysine, within a couple of minutes, they're
gone, so it works quite well.

00:24:52.930 --> 00:24:57.991
We have enzymes and they're quite specific,
so you don't run into the problem, the antibiotic

00:24:57.991 --> 00:25:00.210
problem where you kill everything.

00:25:00.210 --> 00:25:03.840
Your normal floor and the and the organism
you're trying to kill that.

00:25:03.840 --> 00:25:08.040
Quite the, the, the, the staff enzyme will
kill staff.

00:25:08.040 --> 00:25:09.500
Anthrax enzyme kills anthrax.

00:25:09.500 --> 00:25:12.180
So you, you're, you're targeted killing.

00:25:12.180 --> 00:25:14.530
You're not affecting your normal flora.

00:25:14.530 --> 00:25:17.330
So why isn't everybody using lysine?

00:25:17.330 --> 00:25:20.100
I mean, what's the, what are the challenges
that you still face?

00:25:20.100 --> 00:25:23.770
Well, we're in clinical trials right now phase
two.

00:25:23.770 --> 00:25:25.990
Phase one showed that it was quite safe.

00:25:25.990 --> 00:25:27.770
We're in phase two in the hospital.

00:25:27.770 --> 00:25:33.190
So about 117 patients which would sure be
done by the end of this year, treating MRSA

00:25:33.190 --> 00:25:39.100
infections, endocarditis, MRSA, septicemia
and Endocarditis.

00:25:39.100 --> 00:25:40.330
Heart infections?

00:25:40.330 --> 00:25:47.260
Heart valve infections and septicemia, bacteremia
and we'll know by the end of the year.

00:25:47.260 --> 00:25:53.660
So Janelle, I mean you had touched on this
earlier about, you know, maybe paying more

00:25:53.660 --> 00:26:01.740
attention to our own sort of host health in
terms of dealing with these infections and

00:26:01.740 --> 00:26:05.810
you know, you're, you've been doing a lot
of research into, into tolerance.

00:26:05.810 --> 00:26:10.200
Maybe you could sort of describe sort of the
overall idea that you're pursuing and then

00:26:10.200 --> 00:26:15.600
how do you know how that might translate into
an actual treatment for a patient?

00:26:15.600 --> 00:26:16.600
Yeah.

00:26:16.600 --> 00:26:24.750
So I think that the, uh, what is evident to
me with our perspective, uh, in developing

00:26:24.750 --> 00:26:29.620
antibiotics and antibiotic history and the
approaches that have been described by my

00:26:29.620 --> 00:26:36.120
fellow panelists is that they're all based
on the question of how do we kill microbes

00:26:36.120 --> 00:26:40.480
and developing ways to kill microbes.

00:26:40.480 --> 00:26:43.300
And we are approaching this from a different
perspective.

00:26:43.300 --> 00:26:49.910
We actually want to understand what it takes
to enable a patient to return back to a healthy

00:26:49.910 --> 00:26:54.360
state and to survive infectious diseases.

00:26:54.360 --> 00:27:01.540
And um, there's, we, I talked about sepsis
and how in sepsis and this is the case with

00:27:01.540 --> 00:27:06.660
other infectious diseases as well, there're
significant physiological damage that occurs

00:27:06.660 --> 00:27:08.830
and that leads to physiological dysfunction.

00:27:08.830 --> 00:27:14.500
And in order for a patient to return to a
healthy state and to survive an infection,

00:27:14.500 --> 00:27:15.580
they have to be able to.

00:27:15.580 --> 00:27:20.450
You have to be able to alleviate that damage
that's occurred, um, and, and restore the

00:27:20.450 --> 00:27:24.910
patient back to normal physiological function.

00:27:24.910 --> 00:27:29.080
And our assumption is that if we just kill
the pathogen, we should be able to do that,

00:27:29.080 --> 00:27:32.540
but that's not necessarily the case.

00:27:32.540 --> 00:27:37.880
You can have patients where antibiotics are
effective in them, but the physiological damage

00:27:37.880 --> 00:27:42.730
that they've endured kills the patient anyways.

00:27:42.730 --> 00:27:52.310
And so there's, we are taking a variety of
approaches to understand, um, if our body

00:27:52.310 --> 00:27:59.080
encodes ways to protect us from infectious
diseases by promoting health and alleviating

00:27:59.080 --> 00:28:00.220
physiological damage.

00:28:00.220 --> 00:28:05.660
And about 10 years ago now, we discovered
that in addition to our immune system, which

00:28:05.660 --> 00:28:12.630
protects us from infections by killing pathogens,
we've discovered that we encode a distinct

00:28:12.630 --> 00:28:16.559
defense strategy that we call the cooperative
defense system.

00:28:16.559 --> 00:28:21.390
And this is a defense system that, um, is
essential for us to survive infections.

00:28:21.390 --> 00:28:26.950
And it protects us by executing what we call
tolerance mechanisms or disease tolerance

00:28:26.950 --> 00:28:27.950
mechanisms.

00:28:27.950 --> 00:28:34.550
And these are mechanisms that our bodies encode
that alleviate physiological damage during

00:28:34.550 --> 00:28:36.020
microbial interactions.

00:28:36.020 --> 00:28:42.240
And so these are mechanisms that promote our
health without killing the pathogen, so you

00:28:42.240 --> 00:28:49.840
can induce these, um, tolerance responses
in, um, a host and they will be perfectly

00:28:49.840 --> 00:28:55.500
healthy and survive the infection despite
having the pathogen present in their body.

00:28:55.500 --> 00:29:00.480
Um, and we like this approach because this
provides a new avenue for treating infectious

00:29:00.480 --> 00:29:06.710
diseases that will enable us to promote survival
of the patient, but they also, um, in theory

00:29:06.710 --> 00:29:12.179
should be what we call anti-evolution proof,
meaning that pathogens should not evolve resistance

00:29:12.179 --> 00:29:17.710
to such strategies because we're targeting
the, the patient and the physiology that's

00:29:17.710 --> 00:29:23.350
affected by the, um, the infectious disease
without having a negative impact on microbial

00:29:23.350 --> 00:29:24.350
fitness.

00:29:24.350 --> 00:29:30.140
It almost sounds like the microbiome is so,
so complex with hundreds or thousands of species

00:29:30.140 --> 00:29:37.440
that, how would you ever disentangle it well
enough to be able to make it into medicine?

00:29:37.440 --> 00:29:39.170
You have to have a hypothesis.

00:29:39.170 --> 00:29:41.530
You have to conduct clinical trials.

00:29:41.530 --> 00:29:44.500
Clinical trials have advanced cancer therapy.

00:29:44.500 --> 00:29:51.050
They've converted HIV infection from a lethal
disease to a completely treatable disease

00:29:51.050 --> 00:29:54.080
with longterm step-by-step clinical trials.

00:29:54.080 --> 00:29:55.550
That that's what the field needs.

00:29:55.550 --> 00:30:01.640
Of course, that's what we need in to to restore,
to have working antibiotics, to develop new

00:30:01.640 --> 00:30:03.330
antibiotics as well.

00:30:03.330 --> 00:30:04.330
Yep.

00:30:04.330 --> 00:30:05.330
Sean.

00:30:05.330 --> 00:30:09.190
We do similar things with the human microbiome
to do the soil because we look at the molecules

00:30:09.190 --> 00:30:13.380
that these bugs make and we've in fact found
antibiotics that are effective against MRSA.

00:30:13.380 --> 00:30:14.380
So these are.

00:30:14.380 --> 00:30:18.170
These are antibiotics made in our bacteria
living inside of us.

00:30:18.170 --> 00:30:19.990
Coded by the bacteria living inside of us.

00:30:19.990 --> 00:30:21.540
And we're sort of antibiotics factories.

00:30:21.540 --> 00:30:22.620
Yeah, we, yeah.

00:30:22.620 --> 00:30:24.980
We may not need to undergraduates anymore.

00:30:24.980 --> 00:30:30.700
We may just have to mine our own microbiome.

00:30:30.700 --> 00:30:32.660
Or the undergraduates'.

00:30:32.660 --> 00:30:41.960
To add to the complexity, we also have bacterial
phage in our gut and they are modulating.

00:30:41.960 --> 00:30:46.550
So we have phages that are attacking our bacteria
inside of us all the time.

00:30:46.550 --> 00:30:51.000
We eat, drink phage all the time.

00:30:51.000 --> 00:30:57.460
Ten trillion phage pass through our gut into
our tissues everyday, everyday.

00:30:57.460 --> 00:30:58.740
So they're everywhere.

00:30:58.740 --> 00:31:02.360
There's 10th of the 31 phage on earth, so
they're everywhere.

00:31:02.360 --> 00:31:07.549
We eat, drink phage all the time, so they're
in our gut, they're modulating the organism

00:31:07.549 --> 00:31:13.740
up and down, so you have a bloom of phage
and they are killing these particular organism.

00:31:13.740 --> 00:31:17.030
You have a reduction in up in that organism.

00:31:17.030 --> 00:31:22.680
We don't know what physiological effects it
has on our bodies, but it has to have an effect

00:31:22.680 --> 00:31:28.660
and, and understanding the modulation of phage
and our gut flora is a, is another area that

00:31:28.660 --> 00:31:30.110
people are starting to look at.

00:31:30.110 --> 00:31:35.170
And then Janelle, like your own body is then
responding to all these different things going

00:31:35.170 --> 00:31:36.290
on inside of you.

00:31:36.290 --> 00:31:37.290
I mean.

00:31:37.290 --> 00:31:39.260
Absolutely, it's a bi-directional relationship.

00:31:39.260 --> 00:31:49.290
So, um, we're, we're recognizing the microorganisms
that are in our intestine, but also some microbes

00:31:49.290 --> 00:31:54.040
induce host responses or immune responses
to that are not effective against themselves,

00:31:54.040 --> 00:31:57.780
but will be effective against other microbes
within the community.

00:31:57.780 --> 00:32:02.910
So, um, through this, bi-directional communication,
it goes back to ecology 101.

00:32:02.910 --> 00:32:07.510
They're, they're using the host to also shape
that ecosystem.

00:32:07.510 --> 00:32:10.520
So I'm gonna open it up to questions in a
little bit.

00:32:10.520 --> 00:32:17.260
But before I do that, I just wanted to get
a sense from all of you about sort of the

00:32:17.260 --> 00:32:18.260
human side of all of this.

00:32:18.260 --> 00:32:24.880
I mean, we, we talked about how the industry
incentives are all quite perverse and you

00:32:24.880 --> 00:32:30.870
know, it takes time and effort to find these
antibiotics or to develop these other alternatives.

00:32:30.870 --> 00:32:39.730
So are there, do you see changes in a good
direction in terms of, of, you know, creating

00:32:39.730 --> 00:32:47.230
a sort of these scientific or social customs
or, or, or procedures to help get us towards

00:32:47.230 --> 00:32:51.080
this better situation where you might use
the, these things?

00:32:51.080 --> 00:32:57.460
Or are we just going to, you know, like not
be able to define these replacements because

00:32:57.460 --> 00:32:58.970
there isn't enough support for it.

00:32:58.970 --> 00:33:01.430
And Marty, what do you think?

00:33:01.430 --> 00:33:05.880
The bottom line is that we need to be better
stewards of antibiotics.

00:33:05.880 --> 00:33:12.900
We could create 10 new antibiotics or 10 new
lysines, but unless we use them better, uh,

00:33:12.900 --> 00:33:17.059
the, uh, the resistance will get to us.

00:33:17.059 --> 00:33:19.600
The bacteria are, are selected for resistance.

00:33:19.600 --> 00:33:24.120
So we're, we have to reduce the variation
in antibiotic use.

00:33:24.120 --> 00:33:27.660
They're using antibiotics a lot less in Sweden
than they are here.

00:33:27.660 --> 00:33:29.170
People are just as healthy as we are here.

00:33:29.170 --> 00:33:32.740
There's a lot of regional variation in antibiotic
use.

00:33:32.740 --> 00:33:38.960
There's variation from doctor to doctor, the
the practice, the public have to be better

00:33:38.960 --> 00:33:40.610
stewards.

00:33:40.610 --> 00:33:43.350
Understand that antibiotic use has cost.

00:33:43.350 --> 00:33:46.140
We're using it as if it had no cost.

00:33:46.140 --> 00:33:54.850
So you think that we could even now, I mean
not even talking about these amazing possibilities

00:33:54.850 --> 00:33:59.570
that we've just discussed, you think that
we could reduce the amount of antibiotics

00:33:59.570 --> 00:34:02.809
that patients are taking and still be protecting.

00:34:02.809 --> 00:34:04.720
That will help us decrease the pressure.

00:34:04.720 --> 00:34:10.540
You know, one of the questions is why did
C diff move out of the hospital into the community?

00:34:10.540 --> 00:34:14.490
Why did MRSA move from the hospital into the
general community?

00:34:14.490 --> 00:34:16.260
But we might be able to get it back in.

00:34:16.260 --> 00:34:17.260
Right.

00:34:17.260 --> 00:34:18.850
So Janelle you were just nodding before.

00:34:18.850 --> 00:34:19.850
I mean, do you think.

00:34:19.850 --> 00:34:28.540
I think there's some great data from antibiotic
clinical trials from 1920s and 1930s where

00:34:28.540 --> 00:34:35.270
with certain trials, the, the group that received
the placebo, 80 percent of them did just fine.

00:34:35.270 --> 00:34:38.770
We can clear infections on our own.

00:34:38.770 --> 00:34:44.531
We can survive infections on our own, um,
and I think a lot of times by the time a patient

00:34:44.531 --> 00:34:50.510
shows up to the clinic to get the antibiotics,
they, there are studies to suggest that they've

00:34:50.510 --> 00:34:55.409
already cleared the infection and now they're
just getting antibiotics because they have

00:34:55.409 --> 00:34:59.050
some residual symptoms from the infection.

00:34:59.050 --> 00:35:05.091
So I completely agree with Marty that if we
can just temper our use of current, um, antibiotics

00:35:05.091 --> 00:35:07.970
that will help significantly.

00:35:07.970 --> 00:35:09.490
And what about you Sean?

00:35:09.490 --> 00:35:16.800
You were just talking about like, um, how
much money an antibiotic might make and how

00:35:16.800 --> 00:35:18.440
much money is required to do it.

00:35:18.440 --> 00:35:24.830
So like how do you, how do you even, how do
you get those thing numbers to balance out?

00:35:24.830 --> 00:35:31.120
I think the good thing is we've seen this
tremendous effort in the past decade to try

00:35:31.120 --> 00:35:32.390
and solve the discovery problem.

00:35:32.390 --> 00:35:36.990
We still need more money there, but we clearly
see there's global impetus to say we need

00:35:36.990 --> 00:35:37.990
more antibiotics.

00:35:37.990 --> 00:35:42.720
Maybe there're, I think there're 50 recognized
major efforts in the past decade to, to support

00:35:42.720 --> 00:35:45.750
antibiotic discovery internationally.

00:35:45.750 --> 00:35:47.170
So I see that going in the right direction.

00:35:47.170 --> 00:35:48.170
I really do.

00:35:48.170 --> 00:35:50.170
I don't know that it's going to happen fast
enough, we'll ever get enough money, but that's

00:35:50.170 --> 00:35:52.850
in the right direction, but it's the post
antibiotic issue.

00:35:52.850 --> 00:35:54.761
Not only our use, but how do we market it?

00:35:54.761 --> 00:35:56.590
How do we, how do we let those things survive?

00:35:56.590 --> 00:36:00.300
I still think we have a lot of hard thinking
to think about how we're, how we're going

00:36:00.300 --> 00:36:02.609
to do that and I don't think we have, we have
a solution.

00:36:02.609 --> 00:36:05.010
We have great examples we can go to.

00:36:05.010 --> 00:36:09.010
There are lots of things that countries do
to put things in reserve.

00:36:09.010 --> 00:36:11.350
You can say our army is in reserve until we
need it.

00:36:11.350 --> 00:36:12.350
Right?

00:36:12.350 --> 00:36:13.350
We pay a lot of money for that.

00:36:13.350 --> 00:36:16.080
Why not think of same models for antibiotics
that we have them developed.

00:36:16.080 --> 00:36:17.080
They're in reserve.

00:36:17.080 --> 00:36:20.260
We pay for them prior to their use, but before
we need them.

00:36:20.260 --> 00:36:23.020
I mean there's a lot of thought that has to
go into that, but to me that's where the gap

00:36:23.020 --> 00:36:26.990
is at the moment, but we need more money for,
for, for development of antibiotics.

00:36:26.990 --> 00:36:28.790
We see money flooding in.

00:36:28.790 --> 00:36:33.530
We still need more, but there's really still
this question of how do we use them afterwards

00:36:33.530 --> 00:36:36.090
and how do we finance that worries me.

00:36:36.090 --> 00:36:44.540
What might help is the fact that we've been
using for years and decades or a broad spectrum

00:36:44.540 --> 00:36:48.850
antibiotics and they're killing everything
and the reasons for that is when you're sick,

00:36:48.850 --> 00:36:53.070
you go to the hospital and the clinician needs
to know what he's going to treat you with.

00:36:53.070 --> 00:36:56.670
If he doesn't know the bug that's causing
the infection he has to give you something

00:36:56.670 --> 00:36:58.130
that's broad spectrum.

00:36:58.130 --> 00:37:03.320
If we had diagnostics at the bedside, so if
someone comes into the hospital and we know

00:37:03.320 --> 00:37:08.280
exactly what organism's causing the infection
you can treat with an antibiotic that is specific

00:37:08.280 --> 00:37:09.480
for that organism.

00:37:09.480 --> 00:37:13.250
Would have very little effect on your normal
flora, but we're not at that point.

00:37:13.250 --> 00:37:14.250
We're close.

00:37:14.250 --> 00:37:17.869
Our hospitals now can identify the organism
fairly quickly.

00:37:17.869 --> 00:37:21.200
Fairly quickly meaning what kind of time scale?

00:37:21.200 --> 00:37:24.090
Hours, so we're at hours from days to hours.

00:37:24.090 --> 00:37:25.090
And if that.

00:37:25.090 --> 00:37:29.360
If you can do that, then you have antibiotics
that are more channeled to the organism that

00:37:29.360 --> 00:37:32.170
you're killing, which would cause less side
effects.

00:37:32.170 --> 00:37:38.380
And I think that that might be a way to survive
this type of issue that we're having right

00:37:38.380 --> 00:37:39.380
now.

00:37:39.380 --> 00:37:43.850
And then what about sort of uh, these, um,
less, we'll call them less conventional things

00:37:43.850 --> 00:37:46.190
like phage therapy or using lysines or so.

00:37:46.190 --> 00:37:52.310
And do you like in terms of getting a regulatory
approval for these things, do you do, do you

00:37:52.310 --> 00:37:57.000
think that that is able to move forward quickly
enough or.

00:37:57.000 --> 00:38:02.790
Or are we, do we need a better way to sort
of like take in new ideas and try to get them

00:38:02.790 --> 00:38:04.410
approved to be used?

00:38:04.410 --> 00:38:09.430
Well, the lysine therapy has been quite successful
in moving through the system.

00:38:09.430 --> 00:38:15.430
Phage therapy has an issue, because phage
therapy is, is a concoction of many phage

00:38:15.430 --> 00:38:18.150
to control a particular infection.

00:38:18.150 --> 00:38:23.170
And since you could make a cocktail, I can
make a different cocktail that causes the

00:38:23.170 --> 00:38:24.170
same infection.

00:38:24.170 --> 00:38:25.170
There is no IP so there's no incentive.

00:38:25.170 --> 00:38:31.230
The develop phage therapy if it does work
to some degree, but there's no incentive there.

00:38:31.230 --> 00:38:37.260
But if you have a defined molecule then that,
I think that the pathway to get it out out

00:38:37.260 --> 00:38:38.840
the door is quite good.

00:38:38.840 --> 00:38:39.840
Alright.

00:38:39.840 --> 00:38:46.960
So Jo, I'm just curious, are you, when you
look ahead 50 years, do you see the sort of

00:38:46.960 --> 00:38:51.350
the dark picture they recast earlier or do
you, are you optimistic?

00:38:51.350 --> 00:38:52.350
I mean what's.

00:38:52.350 --> 00:38:57.660
I'm always the optimist, but I also have to
be tempered by the fact that we developed

00:38:57.660 --> 00:39:03.420
a plan for antibiotic development and stewardship
when I was in the White House and there were

00:39:03.420 --> 00:39:08.700
some really simple things in there that could
have been done like stewardship of antibiotics

00:39:08.700 --> 00:39:09.700
in hospitals.

00:39:09.700 --> 00:39:13.550
So CDC has an eight point plan of what hospitals
are supposed to do.

00:39:13.550 --> 00:39:18.340
We found that only 50 percent of hospitals
in the United States followed that very, very

00:39:18.340 --> 00:39:24.440
simple plan, like having a strategy for an
antibiotic use in the hospital, training personnel

00:39:24.440 --> 00:39:25.610
in antibiotics.

00:39:25.610 --> 00:39:26.610
It.

00:39:26.610 --> 00:39:30.970
It was really kind of depressing and appalling
and we identified all the things mentioned

00:39:30.970 --> 00:39:36.070
here and then many others that we need to
steward the antibiotics, use them less, have

00:39:36.070 --> 00:39:41.990
better diagnostics so that we know when to
use them and we don't even use the tools today

00:39:41.990 --> 00:39:43.960
that we have like diagnostics.

00:39:43.960 --> 00:39:47.359
I've, I've done this survey completely unscientific.

00:39:47.359 --> 00:39:52.230
I shouldn't even talk about it, but it's my
little way of keeping tabs on the docks.

00:39:52.230 --> 00:39:58.490
I asked in my lab, when people go for a sore
throat, go to the doctor, what do they do?

00:39:58.490 --> 00:40:01.290
And 10 years ago there was never a test.

00:40:01.290 --> 00:40:05.150
They just gave them the antibiotics in every
case.

00:40:05.150 --> 00:40:10.970
And then slowly we started seeing the strep
test, strep throat test being used.

00:40:10.970 --> 00:40:15.550
But even today, fewer than half are getting
a test before they get antibiotics.

00:40:15.550 --> 00:40:17.970
That just seems irresponsible to me.

00:40:17.970 --> 00:40:18.970
Any guesses why?

00:40:18.970 --> 00:40:20.000
I mean Marty, what do you?

00:40:20.000 --> 00:40:21.000
I mean you're the doctor?

00:40:21.000 --> 00:40:22.390
I mean why, what?

00:40:22.390 --> 00:40:25.310
I mean what, it doesn't make sense.

00:40:25.310 --> 00:40:30.800
It's the problem of transparency, the medical
profession and the public overestimate the

00:40:30.800 --> 00:40:37.700
benefit of antibiotic and they underestimate
the cost, the effects of antibiotics.

00:40:37.700 --> 00:40:42.200
And so, uh, we, we have, we have to fix that
and I, I really agree about narrow spectrum

00:40:42.200 --> 00:40:48.970
antibiotics, you know, and, and as I said,
antibiotics are falsely inexpensive, why,

00:40:48.970 --> 00:40:55.150
why give someone a $500 or $5,000 antibiotic
when you can treat them with a $5 antibiotic.

00:40:55.150 --> 00:41:00.940
So the market's broken, but we need to use
tax money just like we need to use tax money

00:41:00.940 --> 00:41:03.950
to buy interstate highways, uh, that, that's
a public.

00:41:03.950 --> 00:41:05.869
Antibiotics are public good.

00:41:05.869 --> 00:41:13.070
We have to invest in, in, in antibiotics that
will protect our future, uh, as, as a public

00:41:13.070 --> 00:41:14.140
good.

00:41:14.140 --> 00:41:17.860
Okay, um, we have microphones.

00:41:17.860 --> 00:41:19.869
A question right in the back there.

00:41:19.869 --> 00:41:21.520
Thank you first off for the presentation,
that was really useful.

00:41:21.520 --> 00:41:23.020
Um, I just have two quick questions.

00:41:23.020 --> 00:41:24.180
So first I'm being.

00:41:24.180 --> 00:41:28.120
So how advanced and what are the, what is
the percentage accuracy on these diagnostic

00:41:28.120 --> 00:41:29.120
tools?

00:41:29.120 --> 00:41:32.170
Can they either be improved or is it just
because these current antibiotics are cheaper,

00:41:32.170 --> 00:41:34.040
they're just not getting that much visibility.

00:41:34.040 --> 00:41:38.170
And my second question is, are there currently
government programs that are in place or in

00:41:38.170 --> 00:41:39.170
the pipeline?

00:41:39.170 --> 00:41:41.990
And the reason I ask this is because there're
orphan diseases out there that don't have

00:41:41.990 --> 00:41:45.420
a large market either, but yet there are a
lot of government programs that incentivize

00:41:45.420 --> 00:41:47.230
the innovations for this space.

00:41:47.230 --> 00:41:50.810
So I'm wondering if that's something that's
happening in the pipeline right now that will

00:41:50.810 --> 00:41:53.220
encourage innovation in the space.

00:41:53.220 --> 00:41:54.220
Great.

00:41:54.220 --> 00:41:55.750
So let's, uh, let's, uh, let's take these
one at a time.

00:41:55.750 --> 00:41:59.320
So Vince maybe you could start us off in terms
of the diagnostics.

00:41:59.320 --> 00:42:04.329
Is it just a case that we already have really
good diagnosis but then they're just not being

00:42:04.329 --> 00:42:05.329
used enough?

00:42:05.329 --> 00:42:10.609
Or are there a possibility to develop new
kinds of technology to really get these things

00:42:10.609 --> 00:42:11.609
identified fast?

00:42:11.609 --> 00:42:13.010
Well, right now they're doing it by DNA analysis.

00:42:13.010 --> 00:42:15.150
So how does that work?

00:42:15.150 --> 00:42:20.780
You just get a number of organisms, a few
organisms from swab and they can take it and

00:42:20.780 --> 00:42:25.280
put it, extract the DNA, put it through a
machine and identify certain genes certain

00:42:25.280 --> 00:42:26.280
pathogens have.

00:42:26.280 --> 00:42:28.260
And they can do that within hours.

00:42:28.260 --> 00:42:32.470
Sometimes they'd have to grow the organism
very for only a few generations to get more

00:42:32.470 --> 00:42:34.910
organisms so they can extract more DNA.

00:42:34.910 --> 00:42:36.410
But it's quite quite accurate.

00:42:36.410 --> 00:42:38.830
It just takes a little more time.

00:42:38.830 --> 00:42:40.320
It's not at the bedside.

00:42:40.320 --> 00:42:43.940
It's a few hours, but it's better than what
we used to have which was overnight.

00:42:43.940 --> 00:42:48.850
We'd have to culture it, let the grow, organisms
grow overnight and then even another test

00:42:48.850 --> 00:42:50.840
usually two days before you get the identification.

00:42:50.840 --> 00:42:55.540
When you say at the bedside, are you saying,
I mean like a doctor comes, a nurse comes

00:42:55.540 --> 00:42:58.770
and takes your temperature at the bedside,
takes your blood pressure at the bedside or

00:42:58.770 --> 00:42:59.770
you're saying-

00:42:59.770 --> 00:43:01.900
Well like a rapid strep test is in a sense
at the bedside.

00:43:01.900 --> 00:43:07.349
You can swab the throat and put it into a
solution that digests the organism and they

00:43:07.349 --> 00:43:11.640
have an antibody that identifies a molecule
on that organism.

00:43:11.640 --> 00:43:12.830
You can do that within 20 minutes.

00:43:12.830 --> 00:43:14.980
You get the results of that experiment.

00:43:14.980 --> 00:43:16.270
That's at the bedside.

00:43:16.270 --> 00:43:19.340
We're not there yet, but we're close.

00:43:19.340 --> 00:43:21.450
And are hospitals like.

00:43:21.450 --> 00:43:27.860
It sounds from your, from your survey, I would
guess that maybe hospitals are a little slow

00:43:27.860 --> 00:43:32.820
to really snap up the best of these diagnostics.

00:43:32.820 --> 00:43:36.070
They are used.

00:43:36.070 --> 00:43:38.150
Some are and some aren't.

00:43:38.150 --> 00:43:43.670
Just like the simple practices to reduce antibiotic
resistance, which don't cost any money and

00:43:43.670 --> 00:43:48.410
in most cases some adopt them and some don't.

00:43:48.410 --> 00:43:49.410
And Marty,

00:43:49.410 --> 00:43:53.650
I mean most antibiotics used in the United
States and most countries are used in outpatients.

00:43:53.650 --> 00:43:59.849
They're not used, so the focus, 90 percent
of the antibiotics are used in outpatient,

00:43:59.849 --> 00:44:05.730
and most of the antibiotics are used for upper
respiratory infections, which we know that

00:44:05.730 --> 00:44:12.410
a big fraction are viral and are not bacterial
at all, so viral infections don't respond

00:44:12.410 --> 00:44:13.410
to antibiotics.

00:44:13.410 --> 00:44:18.750
So we need a rapid diagnostic that will tell
whether that outpatient walking in has a viral

00:44:18.750 --> 00:44:19.840
infection or bacterial infection.

00:44:19.840 --> 00:44:21.730
In the doctor's office as well.

00:44:21.730 --> 00:44:27.990
If we had that tool, we could eliminate a
lot of unnecessary antibiotic testing.

00:44:27.990 --> 00:44:30.540
One of the problems is that the antibiotic
costs $5.

00:44:30.540 --> 00:44:32.280
The test might cost $500.

00:44:32.280 --> 00:44:37.070
So our health system isn't, It's not working.

00:44:37.070 --> 00:44:38.070
Okay.

00:44:38.070 --> 00:44:41.740
So after we get fix antibiotic problem, we'll
fix the healthcare system, right?

00:44:41.740 --> 00:44:44.100
Or first, what if, at the same time.

00:44:44.100 --> 00:44:46.700
Anyway, the second question was about what?

00:44:46.700 --> 00:44:47.970
What are, what are there?

00:44:47.970 --> 00:44:57.700
Are there any special government programs
that are actually like trying to, to push

00:44:57.700 --> 00:45:01.120
research about resistance forward with what's
happening?

00:45:01.120 --> 00:45:07.730
So as, as you mentioned, I'm on this commission
that Jo was involved in setting up called

00:45:07.730 --> 00:45:14.160
PACCARB, which is President Obama set it up
by executive order and, and our mandate is

00:45:14.160 --> 00:45:20.760
to combat antibiotic resistant bacteria and
we have five different areas, surveillance,

00:45:20.760 --> 00:45:26.680
stewardship, new diagnostics, therapeutics
and international efforts working with other

00:45:26.680 --> 00:45:27.680
countries.

00:45:27.680 --> 00:45:32.890
So as, as part of these and, and the executive
order, uh, money's have gone into something

00:45:32.890 --> 00:45:39.830
called BARDA, which is to develop new antibiotics,
to put money in, to make it more economically

00:45:39.830 --> 00:45:45.530
viable, to develop antibodies, to look at
antibiotics like orphan diseases as well.

00:45:45.530 --> 00:45:52.609
They're, they're special stipulations that
make it more attractive for companies to make

00:45:52.609 --> 00:45:55.240
products for orphan diseases.

00:45:55.240 --> 00:45:56.240
Great.

00:45:56.240 --> 00:45:58.990
Any more questions?

00:45:58.990 --> 00:46:04.710
Is prevention still a big thing in terms of
the washing of the hands thing, is that still

00:46:04.710 --> 00:46:06.050
the best thing we can do?

00:46:06.050 --> 00:46:08.570
I have one little version of that.

00:46:08.570 --> 00:46:12.320
I try not to touch anything when I go to the
men's room.

00:46:12.320 --> 00:46:15.240
Does that work?

00:46:15.240 --> 00:46:17.170
So soap and water works.

00:46:17.170 --> 00:46:22.510
There's no question, but on the other hand,
there are all these antibacterial products,

00:46:22.510 --> 00:46:26.420
uh, that are killing the good bacteria.

00:46:26.420 --> 00:46:29.910
Good bacteria help protect us against the
bad bacteria against the invader.

00:46:29.910 --> 00:46:32.100
So are they doing more good or good?

00:46:32.100 --> 00:46:33.100
Doing more harm.

00:46:33.100 --> 00:46:36.630
And I don't know either.

